      "title": "Maryland’s three provider types",
      "narration": "Maryland law recognizes three kinds of organizations that deliver health benefits. An insurer, defined broadly in the Insurance Article, is a person engaged as a principal in the business of insurance. A nonprofit health service plan is organized under Title 14 to provide health services on a prepaid or indemnity basis to subscribers, not for profit. A health maintenance organization combines financing and delivery of care, is authorized under the Health-General Article and must hold a certificate of authority from the Insurance Commissioner, with the Maryland Department of Health overseeing quality. All three must obey Maryland’s mandated benefits and consumer protections.",
